-- title:
--   Hecke coordinate with bounded kernel on 2^m-torsion of multiplicative type
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and let $B$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lying over $2$, meaning $2$ is a non-unit of $B$; let $I = B.inertiaSubgroupIn\ \mathbb{Q}$ be the image in $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ of the inertia subgroup of $B$ under the inclusion of its decomposition subgroup, and give $J_0 =$ `JZero p`, the degree-zero divisor class group of the geometric modular function field of level $p$, its `heckeModuleBar p` action of `HeckeAlg` $= \mathrm{MvPolynomial}\ \mathrm{Nat.Primes}\ \mathbb{Z}$. The assertion is that there is a single $C \in \mathbb{N}$ with the following property for every $m \in \mathbb{N}$ and every function $n$ from $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ to $\mathbb{N}$: suppose $\sigma\zeta = \zeta^{n(\sigma)}$ for all $\sigma$ and all $\zeta$ with $\zeta^{2^m} = 1$, and suppose that on the subgroup $W_m$, defined as the intersection of the $2^m$-torsion of $J_0$ with the subgroup generated by all displacements $\sigma\cdot x - x$ with $\sigma \in I$ and $x$ annihilated by some power $(\mathfrak{p}_2)^M$ of the Eisenstein maximal ideal `eisensteinMaximalIdeal p 2`, every $\sigma \in I$ acts as multiplication by the natural number $n(\sigma)$. Then there exists an additive homomorphism $\iota$ from $W_m$ to the quotient of `heckeLatticeAlgebra p ∅` (the algebra of endomorphisms of the integral lattice in weight-two cusp forms induced by Hecke operators) by the ideal generated by $2^m$, such that $\iota(t \cdot x) = \bar{t}\,\iota(x)$ whenever $t \in$ `HeckeAlg`, $x \in W_m$ and $t \cdot x \in W_m$, where $\bar{t}$ is the class of the image of $t$ under `heckeEvalForms p 2` followed by `latticeRestrictHom p ∅`, and such that $\ker \iota$ is finite of cardinality at most $2^C$.
--
--   The statement supplies a Hecke-equivariant coordinate, of rank one up to a kernel bounded uniformly in $m$, on the part of the $2^m$-torsion of $J_0(p)$ generated by inertial displacements of Eisenstein torsion, under the hypothesis that inertia at $2$ acts there through powers of the cyclotomic character; it is the analogue, at the level of full $2^m$-torsion rather than of $\mathfrak{p}^m$-torsion, of the multiplicative-type analysis in Mazur's study of the Eisenstein ideal. It is used to derive the corresponding bound for the Eisenstein torsion subgroups themselves.
